WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.752
The central intuition behind self-attention is selective information sharing.

00:00:04.752 --> 00:00:12.107
When the model updates the representation of one token, it does not need to treat every other token

00:00:12.107 --> 00:00:12.492
as equally important.

00:00:12.492 --> 00:00:20.037
It can assign larger weights to tokens that are useful for the current representation and smaller weights to

00:00:20.037 --> 00:00:21.832
less relevant positions.

00:00:21.832 --> 00:00:25.912
Imagine the sentence “The animal did not cross the street because it was tired.” When updating the

00:00:25.912 --> 00:00:32.032
representation of the word it, the model may place more weight on animal than on street because animal

00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:34.967
helps resolve the reference.

00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:40.562
The model learns these weighting patterns during training; we do not manually write the rule.

00:00:40.562 --> 00:00:45.907
Attention therefore creates a direct path for information to move between useful tokens.

00:00:45.907 --> 00:00:52.917
Later we will turn this intuition into Query, Key, and Value vectors and then into the attention formula.

00:00:52.917 --> 00:00:58.428
For now, keep the simple idea: each token can decide where to gather context.
